  "paragraphs": [
   "This is prescription-strength, it requires a prescription because it's that strong. Doctor review included after checkout.",
   "The Red Sox training staff needed pain relief their players could rub on mid-game: nothing swallowed, no GI issues, fast enough to work between innings.",
   "So our compounding pharmacy built them one: a ketorolac gel that absorbs in seconds and works right where you put it.",
   "That exact formula is KETRO RX: PAIN GEL. Same active. Same strength. Compounded at an FDA-registered US pharmacy.",
   "Targets acute flare-ups, chronic stiffness, sciatic-area pain",
   "Treats osteoarthritis flares, end-of-hike stiffness, post-run joint pain",
   "Relieves carpal tunnel, tennis elbow, arthritis-stiff hands",
   "Reduces inflammation from pulled muscles, sprains, soft-tissue injury",
   "Eases chronic neck pain, frozen-shoulder ache, pinched-nerve flares",
   "Calms severe clenching, jaw-joint inflammation, chronic facial pain",
   "Not menthol. Not tiger balm. Not a scented lotion pretending to be medicine.",
   "This is 3% prescription Ketorolac, the formula the Boston Red Sox has used for years. Compounded to order, and strong enough to need a doctor's sign-off.",
   "Why does that matter? Menthol distracts you from pain. A prescription NSAID reduces it — at the source. It doesn't mask the pain with a warming sensation. It reduces the inflammation causing it.",
   "Prescription ketorolac, right at the site of pain. Some customers feel relief within hours."
  ]
